            <ArticleTitle>Classic transcrural celiac plexus block simulated on 100 computed tomography images.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>35-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2015.12.018</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(16)00039-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate intra-abdominal needle trajectories of the classic transcrural celiac plexus block (tCPB) with a simulation technique.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Classic tCPB technique cited from 10 latest authoritative textbooks were simulated on abdominal computed tomography images retrospectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-affiliated community hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred axial computed tomography images across the celiac trunk were retrieved. Three lines simulating classic tCPB were executed on each image. The organs traversed by each line were noted and analyzed. The frequencies of organ traverse were compared with the incidences described in the literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All 3 lines traversed various organs with different frequencies. The right side line frequently traversed the right kidney (36%). The left side line always traversed the aorta (100%). The modified line on the right side frequently traversed the inferior vena cava (32%). The highest kidney traverse percentage on both sides and the highest aorta traverse percentage on the right side were observed in pancreatic cancer patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Despite uncommon clinical complications, classic tCPB needle placement frequently traversed through several key organs in this simulation series. Organ penetrations could be avoided by needle trajectory adjustment.</AbstractText>
